@* The three ways a gated page can refuse, in one place.

   **Two pages had all three, word for word.** `AdminOnly` wraps a demonstration and `Admin`
   answers the same question for itself, because it needs the state for its own fetch as well as
   for the gate — so it could not simply be wrapped, and the copy was pasted instead. Three
   sentences in two files is three sentences that drift apart one edit at a time, and nothing in
   the suite could have said so until `NoTwoPagesCarryTheSameSentence`.

   **The "Looking…" branch is deliberately not here.** Each page names what it is looking for, so
   those two lines differ on purpose and are not duplication. *@

@if (State == ListRequest.NotSignedIn)
{
    <Panel Title="Sign in first">
        <p>This page needs an account. <a href="signin">Sign in</a>, then come back.</p>
    </Panel>
}
else if (State == ListRequest.Unavailable)
{
    <Panel Title="Could not reach the site">
        <p>Nothing was reached just now. Try again in a moment.</p>
    </Panel>
}
else if (State == ListRequest.NotForYou)
{
    @* Kept as it was. The audit called this reassurance answering an unasked question; on a
       screen that has just told somebody a page is not for them, "did I break something" is a
       question they are actually asking. *@
    <Panel Title="Not this account">
        <p>Nothing is wrong — there is simply nothing here for this account.</p>
        <p><a href="build">Back to the character generator</a></p>
    </Panel>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>Which refusal to draw. Anything else — including <c>Loaded</c> — draws nothing.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ListRequest? State { get; set; }
}
